#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86221215-32a6-5c5a-bcd7-220e166bcee8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "a0f21b4c-0f5c-5567-aaec-9da481d2e7ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-547" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x) = x^2-2x+2$ on the interval $[0,3]$." :question/worked-solution "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(c) (b-a) & = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx \\\\[5pt] f(c) & = \\dfrac {1}{b-a} \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {1}{3-0} \\int_0^3 (x^2-2x+2) \\, \\textrm dx \\\\[5pt] & = \\left. \\dfrac {1}{3} \\cdot \\left[\\dfrac{x^3}{3} -x^2 +2x\\right] \\right|_0^3 \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {1}{3} \\cdot \\left[ \\left(\\dfrac{3^3}{3} -3^2 +2\\cdot3\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{0^3}{3} -0^2 +2\\cdot0\\right)\\right] \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {1}{3}\\cdot6 \\\\[5pt] & = 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(c) &= 2 \\\\\nc^{2} - 2c + 2 &= 2 \\\\\nc(c - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nc &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55fab3ac-cad3-54bd-b07b-9159554b634c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "0c84fc6d-422e-5729-94d2-a12cda71f78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-548" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x) = x - 2$ on the interval $[-2, 4]$." :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{avg} &= \\frac{1}{b - a}∫_{a}^{b}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= (1)/(4 - (-2))∫_{-2}^{4}x - 2dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - 2x) \\mid _{-2}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}[(\\frac{4^{2}}{2} - 2(4)) - (((-2)^{2})/(2) - 2(-2))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}[(8 - 8) - (2 + 4)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(0 - 6) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bedcde-cbc1-5634-8846-eb58ffda32e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Interval Given the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "244bb10c-4c6f-5be0-bd9a-72b4defee245" :question/math-academy-id "e-549" :question/problem "Find $c$ such that the average value of $g(x) = 3 x$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $3$." :question/worked-solution "To solve for $c$, we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{avg} &= \\frac{1}{b - a}∫_{a}^{b}g(x)dx \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{c - 0}∫_{0}^{c}3xdx \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{c}(\\frac{3x^{2}}{2}) \\mid _{0}^{c} \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{c}(\\frac{3c^{2}}{2} - 0) \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{3c}{2} \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100650 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd9167b4-572f-589f-9b94-b62ba57eb28f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "a1572338-79f7-5da7-b9f6-552dcb7984a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-550" :question/problem "The graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[1,6]$, is shown below. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $2$, respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1203/Source/Images/348b67f3907c98dd642975d127a3ea0a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{6} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -5 + 2 = -3\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{avg} &= \\frac{1}{b - a}∫_{a}^{b}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6 - 1} \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86221215-32a6-5c5a-bcd7-220e166bcee8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc98920-a8a5-5228-b8f7-f520a1ecaadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fc98920-a8a5-5228-b8f7-f520a1ecaadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232878} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8b083ad-82fd-5802-8f58-2135c5954920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099389}] :question/id #uuid "0c9992f2-7c1f-5f91-a855-c99990d2066a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344692" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x) = x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 4$ on the interval $[0, 2]$.$c =$\n{{field-1}}"}]}
